30 Days of Ansible – Day 10/30

Ansible Variables – Write Once, Reuse Everywhere

Imagine you need to install Nginx on Development today and Apache on Production tomorrow.

Would you create two different playbooks?

No! That's where Ansible Variables come in.

Variables allow you to write flexible, reusable, and dynamic playbooks by storing values that can be reused throughout your automation.

Why Use Variables?

✅ Avoid hardcoding values

✅ Write reusable playbooks

✅ Easily manage different environments

✅ Simplify maintenance

✅ Improve readability

Defining Variables

---
- name: Install Web Server
  hosts: webservers
  become: yes

  vars:
    package_name: nginx
    service_name: nginx

  tasks:
    - name: Install package
      package:
        name: "{{ package_name }}"
        state: present

    - name: Start service
      service:
        name: "{{ service_name }}"
        state: started
        enabled: yes

Understanding "{{ }}"

In Ansible, variables are referenced using Jinja2 syntax.

name: "{{ package_name }}"

Ansible replaces "{{ package_name }}" with the actual value during execution.

For example:

package_name: nginx

becomes:

name: nginx

Real-World Example

Suppose your company has three environments:

- Development → Nginx
- Testing → Apache
- Production → Nginx

Instead of maintaining three separate playbooks, you keep one playbook and change only the variable values. This makes your automation cleaner, faster, and easier to maintain.

Best Practices

✅ Use meaningful variable names.

✅ Store environment-specific variables separately.

✅ Avoid hardcoding usernames, package names, and file paths.

✅ Use variables wherever values may change.

Key Takeaway

Variables make your Ansible playbooks dynamic, reusable, and easier to maintain. They are one of the most powerful features for building scalable automation.
